const intro={"n":25,"title":"Forced Frontier Recruitment and the Families Left Behind","nums":[932,938,950],"summary":"Picardía recounts a recruitment drive shortly after the election. An armed police party rounds up men for a contingent bound for the frontier. The commander rebukes each man for different alleged offenses, including not voting, failing to obey summonses or criticizing authorities, and adds him to the list. Mothers, sisters and wives plead for their relatives while the judge claims the decision belongs to the commander. Picardía emphasizes the families' distress and uncertainty.","people":"Picardía as narrator and one of the men awaiting assessment; the police officer; the commander; a judge; recruited men; mothers, sisters and wives.","themes":"Compulsory military service; patronage and political punishment; family separation; official evasion of responsibility; social inequality.","words":"contingente (group recruited for service); leva (compulsory recruitment); partida (armed police party); Comendante (commander); papeleta (document or paper); partido (local district).","questions":["How does the armed party gather men for service?","Which alleged offenses does the commander cite?","What does the judge tell the families seeking relief?","How does the narrator depict mothers and wives during the recruitment?","How does this scene echo Fierro's earlier account of being sent to the frontier?"],"analysis":"The passage explicitly connects the commander's accusations with electoral behavior and other local conflicts. It also portrays the human cost in the families who remain. These are Picardía's reported experiences, not a complete statistical account of nineteenth-century recruitment.","related":"/en/students/canto-3/","relatedTitle":"Fierro's earlier frontier recruitment"};
